Subject: [PATCH 11048/11171] Record that the _no_llm suffix decides whether a
 replay is quarantined

exp6979 is stamped flagged and a fresh linter run reproduces it, so unlike
exp6967 this quarantine is current rather than a stale stamp -- checked the
linter's git log first, per the trap recorded yesterday.

Its substrate, fresh_process_readonly_transaction_replay, resolves to a 60-second
live-inference floor with reason live_model, because it has no recognized
deterministic entry and falls through to the compute-bound-marker branch. At
0.029s it is flagged, mechanically correctly and substantively wrongly.

Sorting the recent replay substrates by outcome shows the discriminator is the
name suffix. Three ending in _no_llm get the 0.0001s floor and pass. Three
without it get 60 seconds; one is flagged and two are at risk, including exp6968
whose substrate is fresh_process_replay_of_frozen_live_agent_engine.

The linter's no_llm_name_suffix path is deliberately name-shaped so the match
stays auditable rather than reading as an allowlist, which is defensible. The
consequence looks unintended: the same work, named without the suffix, is
quarantined, and an agent picks its quarantine status when it picks a string with
nothing telling it so.

Not fixed and explicitly not by allowlist, which is both forbidden here and would
defeat the auditable-by-name design deliberately. Either task prompts require the
suffix on any substrate that loads no model, or the linter recognises
deterministic replay by shape. Both change what the corpus is judged against.

Written because I made this claim in passing during a status check -- that the
substrate did not resolve a known floor -- and the rule is to verify such a claim
or drop it. Verified.

